    week: 6,
    foodFocus: "By now you have had five weeks of trying different breakfasts, lunches and dinners, using leftovers when they help, pulling emergency meals together when life gets messy and working out which foods actually suit you. This week is about what you are going to keep. You do not need 100 recipes or a perfect meal plan. You need a handful of breakfasts that work on your mornings, lunches you can organise without too much effort, dinners the household will eat, a few things worth keeping in the freezer and some backup meals for the days when cooking is simply not happening. Think back over the last five weeks. Which meals did you look forward to? Which ones were easy enough that you would make them again? Which lunches kept you going? Which dinners worked for everyone? What was worth making extra of, and what did you discover you really do not care for? Some weeks will be organised and some will not. There will be takeaway, birthdays, weekends away, late nights, work functions, empty fridges and plans that change at the last minute. None of those things mean the week has gone off track. The skill is getting back to your usual meals at the next opportunity instead of deciding the whole week has gone off track. Plan A is great when it works. Plan B and Plan C are what keep things moving when it does not.",
